  "raw_text": "Step 1: To find the maximum value of \\( ab^2 c^3 d^4 \\) given the constraint \\( a + b + c + d = 10 \\), we can use the method of Lagrange multipliers or apply the AM-GM inequality directly for simplicity.\n\nStep 2: Apply the AM-GM inequality to the terms \\( a, \\frac{b}{2}, \\frac{b}{2}, \\frac{c}{3}, \\frac{c}{3}, \\frac{c}{3}, \\frac{d}{4}, \\frac{d}{4}, \\frac{d}{4}, \\frac{d}{4} \\).\n\nStep 3: According to AM-GM, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{a + \\frac{b}{2} + \\frac{b}{2} + \\frac{c}{3} + \\frac{c}{3} + \\frac{c}{3} + \\frac{d}{4} + \\frac{d}{4} + \\frac{d}{4} + \\frac{d}{4}}{10} \\geq \\sqrt[10]{a \\cdot \\left(\\frac{b}{2}\\right)^2 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{c}{3}\\right)^3 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{d}{4}\\right)^4}\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Simplify the left side of the inequality:\n\\[\n\\frac{a + b + c + d}{10} = \\frac{10}{10} = 1\n\\]\n\nStep 5: Therefore, we get:\n\\[\n1 \\geq \\sqrt[10]{a \\cdot \\left(\\frac{b}{2}\\right)^2 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{c}{3}\\right)^3 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{d}{4}\\right)^4}\n\\]\n\nStep 6: Raise both sides to the power of 10 to eliminate the root:\n\\[\n1^{10} \\geq a \\cdot \\left(\\frac{b}{2}\\right)^2 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{c}{3}\\right)^3 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{d}{4}\\right)^4\n\\]\n\nStep 7: This simplifies to:\n\\[\n1 \\geq a \\cdot \\frac{b^2}{4} \\cdot \\frac{c^3}{27} \\cdot \\frac{d^4}{256}\n\\]\n\nStep 8: Multiply both sides by \\( 4 \\cdot 27 \\cdot 256 \\):\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 27 \\cdot 256 \\geq a \\cdot b^2 \\cdot c^3 \\cdot d^4\n\\]\n\nStep 9: Calculate the product on the left side:\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 27 \\cdot 256 = 27648\n\\]\n\nStep 10: Thus, we have:\n\\[\n27648 \\geq ab^2 c^3 d^4\n\\]\n\nStep 11: The equality in AM-GM holds when all the terms are equal, i.e., \\( a = \\frac{b}{2} = \\frac{c}{3} = \\frac{d}{4} \\).\n\nStep 12: Let \\( a = k, \\frac{b}{2} = k, \\frac{c}{3} = k, \\frac{d}{4} = k \\). Then \\( b = 2k, c = 3k, d = 4k \\).\n\nStep 13: Substitute these into the sum constraint:\n\\[\nk + 2k + 3k + 4k = 10\n\\]\n\\[\n10k = 10\n\\]\n\\[\nk = 1\n\\]\n\nStep 14: So, \\( a = 1, b = 2, c = 3, d = 4 \\).\n\nStep 15: Substitute these values back into \\( ab^2 c^3 d^4 \\):\n\\[\n1 \\cdot 2^2 \\cdot 3^3 \\cdot 4^4 = 1 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 27 \\cdot 256 = 27648\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{27648}<|im_end|>",
